N2  - MAGE formalizes a largely informal and experience-led decision-making process in small satellite anomaly management. It is especially useful when only limited telemetry data is available or novice groups of engineers, such as small-satellite university programs, encounter anomalies. These groups tend to be resource constrained in schedule, budget, and expertise. By leveraging hybrid model-based methods and logical reasoning, MAGE provides a structured, formalized, high-level approach to fault detection, diagnosis, and resolution that can be navigated effectively by novice and experienced operators. This comprehensive method extends beyond traditional fault trees, addressing the limitations posed by incomplete information and the potential biases of past experiences. The classification of anomalies is especially useful for understanding and resolving them. An existing spacecraft mission was used to validate MAGE. The results of which illustrate the practical application and benefits of MAGE, showcasing its ability to work with abstract models and limited data. Thus, this tool enables novice teams to manage spacecraft anomalies and potentially save their missions from failure. The versatility of MAGE and its integration of operator intent further enhance its utility, making it a valuable tool to ensure the success and reliability of future space missions.
